.\" scanf.3 - scanf library function family
.TH SCANF 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vscanf, vfscanf, vsscanf \- input format conversion
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int scanf(const char *restrict " format ", ...);"
.br
.BI "int fscanf(FILE *restrict " stream ", const char *restrict " format ", ...);"
.br
.BI "int sscanf(const char *restrict " str ", const char *restrict " format ", ...);"
.PP
.B #include <stdarg.h>
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int vscanf(const char *restrict " format ", va_list " ap );
.br
.BI "int vfscanf(FILE *restrict " stream ", const char *restrict " format ", va_list " ap );
.br
.BI "int vsscanf(const char *restrict " str ", const char *restrict " format ", va_list " ap );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR scanf ()
family of functions scans formatted input according to
.IR format
as described below.
This format may contain
.IR "conversion specifications" ;
the results from such conversions, if any, are stored in the locations
pointed to by the
.I pointer arguments
that follow
.IR format .
Each pointer argument must be of a type appropriate for the value
returned by the corresponding conversion specification.
.PP
.BR scanf ()
reads input from the standard input stream
.IR stdin .
.BR fscanf ()
reads input from the stream pointer
.IR stream .
.BR sscanf ()
reads its input from the null-terminated character string pointed to
by
.IR str ;
reaching the terminating
.B \(aq\e0\(aq
is treated as end-of-input.
.PP
The
.BR vscanf (),
.BR vfscanf (),
and
.BR vsscanf ()
functions are analogous to
.BR scanf (),
.BR fscanf (),
and
.BR sscanf ()
respectively, except that they take a
.I va_list
in place of a variable number of arguments.  The caller is responsible
for invoking
.BR va_start (3)
before the call and
.BR va_end (3)
afterwards.  None of these routines invokes
.BR va_end (3)
itself.
.SS Format of the format string
The format string is a sequence of
.I directives
which are interpreted in order.  A directive is one of the following:
.IP \(bu 3
A sequence of white-space characters (space, tab, newline, etc.; see
.BR isspace (3)).
This directive matches any amount of white space, including none, in the
input.  It never causes a matching failure.
.IP \(bu 3
An ordinary character (i.e., one other than white space or
.BR \(aq%\(aq ).
This character must exactly match the next character of input; if it
does not, the directive fails and the differing input character is left
unread.
.IP \(bu 3
A
.IR "conversion specification" ,
which commences with a
.B \(aq%\(aq
(percent) character.  A sequence of characters from the input is
converted according to this specification, and (unless assignment was
suppressed) the result is placed in the corresponding pointer argument.
If the input does not match the specification, the directive fails:
this is a
.IR "matching failure" .
If input is exhausted before a directive can match, the directive fails:
this is an
.IR "input failure" .
.SS Conversion specifications
Each conversion specification in
.I format
has the following form:
.PP
.RS 4
.B %[*][\fIwidth\fR][\fIlength\fR]\fIspecifier\fR
.RE
.PP
A conversion specification is composed of the following components, in
order:
.TP
.B *
An optional
.IR "assignment-suppression character" .
.BR scanf ()
reads input as directed by the conversion specification but discards
the input.  No corresponding pointer argument is consumed, and the
successful conversion is not added to the assignment count returned by
.BR scanf ().
.TP
.I width
An optional decimal integer greater than zero that specifies the
.IR "maximum field width" .
At most
.I width
characters are read from the input when performing the conversion;
fewer may be read if a matching failure or input failure terminates
scanning earlier (or if a white-space character is encountered for
conversions that stop at white space).  The width count includes
characters that the conversion would otherwise discard, such as a
leading sign or a
.B 0x
prefix.  For
.B %c
the field width specifies the exact number of characters to read; if
no width is given,
.B 1
is used.  A field width of zero is invalid.
.TP
.I length
An optional length modifier that specifies the size of the destination
object.  See
.B "Length modifiers"
below.
.TP
.I specifier
A single conversion specifier character that selects the conversion to
be performed.  See
.B "Conversion specifiers"
below.
.PP
Except for
.BR %c ,
.BR %[ ,
.BR %n ,
and the literal
.B %%
directive, every conversion first skips leading white-space
characters in the input (as if by
.BR isspace (3)).
The skipped white space does not count toward the field width and is
never an input or matching failure on its own.
.SS Length modifiers
The following
.I length
modifiers may appear between the field width and the conversion
specifier; they alter the type of the corresponding pointer argument
for the integer, floating-point, and
.B %n
conversions.
.TP
.B hh
For
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
or
.BR n ,
the argument is a pointer to
.I signed char
or
.IR "unsigned char" .
.TP
.B h
For
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
or
.BR n ,
the argument is a pointer to
.I short
or
.IR "unsigned short" .
.TP
.B l
For integer conversions, the argument is a pointer to
.I long
or
.IR "unsigned long" .
For floating-point conversions
.RB ( e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
.BR a ,
.BR A ),
the argument is a pointer to
.IR double .
.TP
.B ll
For
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
or
.BR n ,
the argument is a pointer to
.I "long long"
or
.IR "unsigned long long" .
.TP
.B j
The argument is a pointer to
.I intmax_t
or
.IR uintmax_t .
.TP
.B z
The argument is a pointer to
.I size_t
(or, for signed conversions, the corresponding signed type
.IR ssize_t ).
.TP
.B t
The argument is a pointer to
.IR ptrdiff_t .
.TP
.B L
For floating-point conversions, the argument is a pointer to
.IR "long double" .
.PP
If a length modifier is supplied that does not match the conversion
specifier (for example,
.B %Ld
or
.BR %hf ),
the result is undefined; the Substrate implementation silently uses
.I int
or
.I float
(as appropriate) for the unrecognized combination.
.SS Conversion specifiers
The following conversion specifiers are supported:
.TP
.B d
Matches an optionally signed decimal integer; the next pointer must
be a pointer to
.I int
(or, with a length modifier, the corresponding integer type).
.TP
.B i
Matches an optionally signed integer; the next pointer must be a
pointer to
.I int
(or, with a length modifier, the corresponding integer type).
The integer is read in base 16 if it begins with
.B 0x
or
.BR 0X ,
in base 8 if it begins with
.BR 0 ,
and in base 10 otherwise.  Only digits valid for the chosen base are
consumed.
.TP
.B o
Matches an unsigned octal integer; the next pointer must be a pointer
to
.I unsigned int
(or, with a length modifier, the corresponding unsigned type).  The
input is interpreted in base 8 regardless of any leading
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B u
Matches an unsigned decimal integer; the next pointer must be a pointer
to
.I unsigned int
(or, with a length modifier, the corresponding unsigned type).
A leading sign is permitted and is interpreted by the underlying
.BR strtoull (3)
conversion.
.TP
.BR x ", " X
Matches an unsigned hexadecimal integer; the next pointer must be a
pointer to
.I unsigned int
(or, with a length modifier, the corresponding unsigned type).  The
optional prefix
.B 0x
or
.B 0X
is recognized and consumed.  Both
.B %x
and
.B %X
behave identically on input.
.TP
.B p
Matches an implementation-defined pointer representation, in the same
form produced by
.BR printf (3)
.BR %p ;
the next pointer must be a pointer to
.IR "void *" .
On Substrate the input is parsed as an unsigned hexadecimal integer
with an optional
.B 0x
prefix.  No sign is accepted.
.TP
.BR f ", " F ", " e ", " E ", " g ", " G
Matches an optionally signed floating-point number, optionally
followed by an exponent (introduced by
.B e
or
.BR E ).
The next pointer must be a pointer to
.I float
(default), to
.I double
(if the
.B l
length modifier is supplied), or to
.I long double
(if the
.B L
modifier is supplied).
The conversions
.BR f / F ,
.BR e / E ,
and
.BR g / G
are equivalent on input.
.TP
.BR a ", " A
Matches an optionally signed hexadecimal floating-point number of the
form
.B [\(+-]0x\fIh\fR.\fIh\fRp[\(+-]\fId\fR
(see
.BR strtod (3)).
If the input does not parse as a hexadecimal floating-point number the
implementation falls back to a decimal floating-point parse, matching
the behaviour of C99
.BR strtod (3).
The pointer type follows the same rules as for the other floating-point
conversions.
.TP
.B c
Matches a sequence of characters whose length is specified by the
.IR width
field
.RB ( 1
if no width is supplied).  The next pointer must be a pointer to
.I char
large enough to receive the bytes; no terminating null byte is added.
The usual leading white-space skip is
.IR "not"
performed.
.TP
.B s
Matches a sequence of non-white-space characters.  The next pointer
must be a pointer to
.I char
with enough room for the matched sequence
.I and
a terminating null byte, which is added automatically.  Leading
white space is skipped before scanning begins, and scanning stops at
the first white-space character (which is left in the input) or when
the field width is exhausted.
.TP
.BI [ scanset ]
Matches a non-empty sequence of characters from the
.IR scanset .
The next pointer must be a pointer to
.I char
with enough room for the matched sequence
.I and
a terminating null byte, which is added automatically.  The usual
leading white-space skip is
.IR "not"
performed.  See
.B "Scanset specifiers"
below for the syntax of
.IR scanset .
.TP
.B n
Stores the number of characters consumed from the input so far through
the pointer argument; the next pointer must be a pointer to
.I int
(or to the type implied by the length modifier).  No input is read,
no input failure can occur, and the assignment is
.IR "not"
counted in the return value.  When combined with
.B *
the assignment is suppressed (and is therefore a no-op).
.TP
.B %
Matches a single
.B \(aq%\(aq
character in the input.  No conversion or assignment occurs; the
specification is
.B %%
in the format string.
.SS Scanset specifiers
A
.BI [ scanset ]
conversion matches characters drawn from
.IR scanset ,
a list of characters bracketed between
.B \(aq[\(aq
and
.BR \(aq]\(aq .
The list defines a set of characters; matching stops at the first
input character not in the set.  At least one character must be matched
or the conversion fails.
.PP
The following syntactic rules apply to
.IR scanset :
.IP \(bu 3
If the first character of
.I scanset
is
.BR \(aq^\(aq ,
the sense of the set is inverted: the conversion matches characters
.IR "not"
in the set.
.IP \(bu 3
If
.B \(aq]\(aq
appears as the first character of the set (or as the first character
after a leading
.BR \(aq^\(aq ),
it is treated as a literal
.B \(aq]\(aq
character in the set, not as the closing bracket.  The set is then
terminated by the next
.BR \(aq]\(aq .
.IP \(bu 3
A range of characters of the form
.IB a - b
specifies all characters between
.I a
and
.I b
inclusive, in ASCII collating order.  The set
.B [a-z]
matches every lowercase letter; the set
.B [^0-9]
matches every character that is not a decimal digit.  If
.I a
is greater than
.IR b ,
the range is interpreted in the reverse order so that the same set is
constructed; this differs from C99, which leaves such a range
unspecified.
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B \(aq-\(aq
appearing as the first or last character of the set (or immediately
after a leading
.BR \(aq^\(aq )
matches a literal hyphen.
.PP
The
.B %[
conversion does
.I not
skip leading white space; if the first input character is not in the
set, the conversion fails immediately and the offending character is
left unread.
.SS Whitespace handling
A white-space directive in
.I format
matches zero or more white-space characters in the input and never
fails.  Conversions
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
.BR a ,
.BR A ,
.BR p ,
and
.BR s
each consume any leading white space before parsing.  The conversions
.BR c ,
.BR [ ,
and
.B n
do
.I not
skip leading white space.  Once a conversion begins, white space is
treated as a normal character and is recognized only as the terminator
of conversions that explicitly stop at it (currently only
.BR s ).
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the number of input items
successfully matched and assigned, which can be fewer than provided
for, or even zero in the event of an early matching failure.  The
.B %n
conversion and the
.B %%
directive do not contribute to the count, and a conversion suppressed
with
.B *
is not counted even though it consumed input.
.PP
The value
.B EOF
is returned if an input failure occurs before the first conversion
(if any) has completed; in particular, if end-of-file is reached
before any input could be matched, the return value is
.BR EOF .
.PP
The macro
.B EOF
is defined in
.IR <stdio.h> .
.SH ERRORS
The
.BR scanf ()
family does not define a fixed set of errors of its own, but it can
fail because of any error returned by the underlying I/O routines:
.TP
.B EAGAIN
The file descriptor underlying
.I stream
has been marked non-blocking
.RB ( O_NONBLOCK )
and the read would block.
.TP
.B EBADF
The file descriptor underlying
.I stream
is not a valid descriptor open for reading.
.TP
.B EILSEQ
The input contains an invalid character sequence.
.TP
.B EINTR
The read operation was interrupted by a signal before any data was
read; see
.BR signal (7).
.TP
.B EINVAL
The conversion specification is invalid (for example, a width of zero
or an unrecognized specifier).
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to perform an internal allocation.
.PP
.BR sscanf ()
operates on a memory buffer and never sets
.IR errno
itself; only the format-string parser can fail, and that failure is
reported by returning a value less than the expected number of
conversions (or
.B EOF
if input is empty).
