.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Parsing several integers from one line
The following program reads three space-separated integers from
.I stdin
and reports how many it actually managed to assign:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    int a, b, c;
    int n;

    n = scanf("%d %d %d", &a, &b, &c);
    if (n == EOF) {
        fprintf(stderr, "input failure before any conversion\en");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("matched %d value(s)\en", n);
    if (n >= 1) printf("a = %d\en", a);
    if (n >= 2) printf("b = %d\en", b);
    if (n >= 3) printf("c = %d\en", c);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
With the input
.B "12 0x1A 077"
the program prints
.BR "matched 3 value(s)" ,
.BR "a = 12" ,
.BR "b = 26" ,
.BR "c = 77" :
.B %d
parses
.B 12
in base 10,
.B %d
on
.B 0x1A
matches only the leading
.B 0
(the
.B x
is not a decimal digit and is left in the input), and parsing of
.B b
stops there.  Replacing
.B %d
with
.B %i
makes the second field parse as hexadecimal and the third as octal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
n = scanf("%i %i %i", &a, &b, &c);   /* "12 0x1A 077" -> 12, 26, 63 */
.EE
.RE
.SS Extracting a substring with a scanset
A common idiom is to read everything up to (but not including) some
delimiter.  The following program reads a
.IR "key=value"
line and stores the key and the value as separate strings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    char key[64];
    char value[256];
    int n;

    /*
     * %63[^=]   key:    up to 63 chars, anything but '='
     *  =        literal: the '=' separator
     *  %255[^\en] value:  up to 255 chars, anything but newline
     */
    n = scanf("%63[^=]=%255[^\en]", key, value);

    if (n == 2)
        printf("key=<%s> value=<%s>\en", key, value);
    else
        fprintf(stderr, "parse failed (matched %d)\en", n);

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
For the input
.B "PATH=/bin:/usr/bin"
the program prints
.BR "key=<PATH> value=</bin:/usr/bin>" .
The negated scansets stop at the appropriate delimiter without
consuming it; because
.B %[
does not skip leading white space, this idiom also handles keys that
contain spaces.
.PP
To match a literal
.B \(aq]\(aq
inside a scanset, place it first:
.B "%[]abc]"
matches any of
.BR \(aq]\(aq ,
.BR \(aq a\(aq ,
.BR \(aq b\(aq ,
or
.BR \(aq c\(aq .
To match a literal
.BR \(aq-\(aq ,
place it first or last:
.B "%[-+0-9]"
matches sign characters and digits.
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR scanf (),
.BR fscanf (),
.BR sscanf (),
.BR vscanf (),
.BR vfscanf (),
and
.BR vsscanf ()
functions conform to ISO C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The
.B hh ,
.B ll ,
.B j ,
.B z ,
and
.B t
length modifiers, the
.B a / A
hexadecimal floating-point conversions, and the
.B v
forms were added in C99.  The treatment of an inverted range
.RB ( a-b
with
.IR a " > " b )
is a Substrate extension; portable code should not rely on it.
